## Git workflow

- Branch naming: `feat/<thing>`, `fix/<thing>`, `chore/<thing>`
- Never commit directly to `main`
- Squash on merge; delete branch after
- Never use `--no-verify`, `--force` (without explicit ask), or `reset --hard`
- See the team-wide methodology in [Hornero](https://github.com/<your-org>/hornero) `methodology.md`

## Testing discipline

<Project-specific testing rules. Default starter:>

- Every behavior change ships with a test (RED → GREEN → REFACTOR)
- Bug fixes start with a failing test that reproduces the bug
- E2E tests are reserved for critical-path slices; everything else lives lower in the pyramid
- Flaky tests are fixed, not retried

## Slash commands and the (currently) one role

Hornero currently ships PM-only. The multi-role-review scaffolding remains in place but is dormant; every fan-out resolves to a single PM lens.

Slash commands available (definitions are skills at `<path-to-hornero>/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`):

- `/orchestrate <task>` — PM as cross-functional orchestrator (marquee for launches/cross-functional decisions).
- `/role <name> <task>` — single named role.
- `/kickoff` — new-user walkthrough.
- `/catalog` — what Hornero ships.
- `/extract-lesson` — end-of-session capture.
- `/audit` — quarterly rot review.

Available role in Hornero:

- `product-manager` — PRDs, prioritisation, success metrics, positioning, JTBD, strategy kernel, bet portfolio, problem framing, interview synthesis, competitive teardown, OKRs, revenue architecture, linear triage.

(The retired roles — software-engineer, designer, tester, security-expert, marketing-expert, data-scientist — return on re-expansion. See Hornero's `ROADMAP.md`.)

